    "english": "In Korean culture, the Milky Way is far more than an astronomical phenomenon — it is a sacred river deeply intertwined with mythology, folk belief, and shamanic tradition. The most celebrated legend is that of Gyeonu and Jiknyeo (Cowherd and Weaver Girl). Jiknyeo, the diligent granddaughter of the Jade Emperor who managed the heavenly weaving, was wed to the cowherd Gyeonu across the celestial river. When the newlyweds became so absorbed in marital bliss that they neglected their duties, the furious Jade Emperor separated them with the Milky Way, allowing them to meet only once a year on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month (Chilseok). On that day, magpies and crows are said to fly to heaven and form the Magpie Bridge (오작교) by joining their heads. This legend transformed the Milky Way into a river of separation, longing, and endurance, and gave birth to the tradition of celebrating Chilseok as a day for lovers. In Korean shamanism (무속신앙), the Milky Way was conceived as the boundary between the living world and the afterlife: the soul of the deceased must cross it to reach the hereafter. The ritual robe worn by shamans (무당) during rites is called 'eunhasu mongduri' (은하수몽두리, Milky Way robe), directly reflecting this belief. Called 천하 (Heavenly River) or 천강 (Heavenly Stream), the Milky Way was seen as the path upon which the divine walked. Joseon dynasty literati composed poetry on the Milky Way theme around Chilseok, and the Magpie Bridge became a key literary symbol of impossible love and miraculous reunion in Korean classical literature, most famously in Chunhyangjeon."
  },
